{"cuisine/cuisine-of-eswatini":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-eswatini","n":"Eswatini cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of eSwatini","intro":[["The cuisine of Eswatini is largely determined by the seasons and the geographical region. Staple foods in Eswatini include sorghum and maize, often served with goat meat. The farming industry mainly depends on sugarcane, tobacco, rice, maize, peanuts, and the export of goat meat and beef. Many Swazis are subsistence farmers who supplement their diet with food bought from markets."],["Produce and imports from coastal nations are also part of the cuisine of Eswatini. Some local markets have food stalls with traditional Swazi meat stew, sandwiches, maize meal and seasonal roasted corn on the cob."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-eswatini"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Eswatini"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"African cuisine"},"cuisine/malian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/malian-cuisine","n":"Malian cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Mali","intro":[["Cuisine in Mali includes rice and millet as staples of Mali, a food culture heavily based on cereal grains. Grains are generally prepared with sauces made from edible leaves, such as spinach, sweet potato or baobab, with tomato peanut sauce. The dishes may be accompanied by pieces of grilled meat (typically chicken, mutton, beef, or goat)."],["Malian cuisine varies regionally. Part of West African cuisine, other popular dishes in Mali include  fufu, Dibi, Jollof rice, and maafe (peanut butter sauce). The National dish is Tiguadege Na, a creamy peanut stew with various meats like beef, chicken or meatballs."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"malian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"African cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/4c/Jollof_rice_with_vegetable.jpg/500px-Jollof_rice_with_vegetable.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Segun Famisa","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jollof_rice_with_vegetable.jpg"}},"cuisine/lanna-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/lanna-cuisine","n":"Lanna cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Thailand","intro":[],"src":null,"wiki":"Lanna cuisine","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"lanna-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lanna_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Lanna: Recipes from Northern Thailand & Beyond","ref":"cookbook/lanna-recipes-from-northern-thailand-beyond","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Chariya Khattiyot","note":"Northern Thai dishes by a chef from the Lanna region.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781788797146","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781788797146"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/nauruan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/nauruan-cuisine","n":"Nauruan cuisine","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Oceania","cuisines#oceania"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Nauru","intro":[["The cuisine of Nauru is the traditional cuisine of the island state on the Pacific Ocean."],["Nauru has the world's highest rate of obesity."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"nauruan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nauruan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"Oceanic cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f1/Coconuts_-_single_and_cracked_open.jpg/500px-Coconuts_-_single_and_cracked_open.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Ivar Leidus","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coconuts_-_single_and_cracked_open.jpg"}},"ingredient/kirsch":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/kirsch","n":"Kirsch","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Kirschwasser, or just Kirsch (German: [kɪʁʃ]; the term used in Switzerland and France, less so in Germany), is a clear, colourless brandy from Germany, Switzerland, and France, traditionally made from double distillation of morello cherries. It is now also made from other kinds of cherries. The cherry stones are included in the fermentation process, not removed beforehand. Unlike cherry liqueurs and cherry brandies, Kirschwasser is not sweet. It is sometimes distilled from fermented cherry juice."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-kirsch"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kirsch"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Swiss cuisine","cuisine/swiss-cuisine",[["Basler Läckerli","dish/basler-lackerli"],["Zuger Kirschtorte","dish/zuger-kirschtorte"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Hazelnut","ingredient/hazelnut"],["Candied fruit","ingredient/candied-fruit"],["Candied peel","ingredient/candied-peel"],["Honey","ingredient/honey"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Butter cream","ingredient/butter-cream"],["Kirschwasser","ingredient/kirschwasser"],["Meringue","ingredient/meringue"],["Sponge cake","ingredient/sponge-cake"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/4/4e/Fuersteneck_Kirschwasser.jpg","w":500,"h":1072,"by":"User:Mahlum","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fuersteneck_Kirschwasser.jpg"}},"ingredient/rice-noodles":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/rice-noodles","n":"Rice noodles","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 5 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Rice noodles are noodles made with rice flour and water as the principal ingredients. Sometimes ingredients such as tapioca or corn starch are added in order to improve the transparency or increase the gelatinous and chewy texture of the noodles. Rice noodles are most common in the cuisines of China, India and Southeast Asia. They are available fresh, frozen, or dried, in various shapes, thicknesses and textures. Fresh noodles are also highly perishable; their shelf life may be just several days."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-rice-noodles"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rice_noodles"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine",[["Boat noodles","dish/boat-noodles"],["Mi krop","dish/mi-krop"],["Pad see ew","dish/pad-see-ew"],["Pad thai","dish/pad-thai"],["Rice noodle roll","dish/rice-noodle-roll"]]],["Cantonese cuisine","cuisine/cantonese-cuisine",[["Rice noodle roll","dish/rice-noodle-roll"]]],["Hong Kong cuisine","cuisine/hong-kong-cuisine",[["Rice noodle roll","dish/rice-noodle-roll"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bean sprouts","ingredient/bean-sprouts"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Dark soy sauce","ingredient/dark-soy-sauce"],["Fermented bean curd","ingredient/fermented-bean-curd"],["Morning glory","ingredient/morning-glory"],["Paprika","ingredient/paprika"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Pickled bean curd","ingredient/pickled-bean-curd"],["Pig blood","ingredient/pig-blood"],["Radish","ingredient/radish"],["Rice vermicelli","ingredient/rice-vermicelli"],["Gai lan","ingredient/gai-lan"],["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/6f/Rice_vermicelli.jpg/500px-Rice_vermicelli.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Popo le Chien","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rice_vermicelli.jpg"}},"dish/borscht":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/borscht","n":"Borscht","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 7 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Borscht is a sour soup, made with meat stock, vegetables and seasonings, common in Eastern Europe, Central Europe and Northern Asia. In English, the word borscht, borrowed via Yiddish, is most often associated with the variant of the soup originating in Ukraine, made with red beetroots as one of the main ingredients, which give the dish its distinctive red color. The same name, however, is also used for a wide selection of sour-tasting soups without beetroots, such as sorrel-based green borscht, rye-based white borscht, and cabbage borscht."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-borscht"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borscht"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Belarusian cuisine","cuisine/belarusian-cuisine"],["Lithuanian cuisine","cuisine/lithuanian-cuisine"],["Moldovan cuisine","cuisine/moldovan-cuisine"],["Polish cuisine","cuisine/polish-cuisine"],["Romanian cuisine","cuisine/romanian-cuisine"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":7,"links":[["Beetroot","ingredient/beetroot"],["Beta vulgaris","ingredient/beta-vulgaris"],["Brassica oleracea","ingredient/brassica-oleracea"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Polonist","ref":"site/polonist-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"polonist.com","note":"Polish recipes and food culture from a Polish-born writer living abroad.","url":"https://www.polonist.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":